WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.609
A pendulum, struck once, swings, and the swing slowly dies away.

00:06.200 --> 00:09.020
Now put it out in the wind, with a gust every

00:09.120 --> 00:13.045
period. It never settles, and it never runs away. Its position

00:13.145 --> 00:17.004
is a stationary time series, and its memory is the pendulum's

00:17.104 --> 00:20.963
own: each gust is remembered for a while, and then forgotten.

00:22.033 --> 00:24.655
That memory, how to read it from data, and how

00:24.755 --> 00:27.732
to forecast with it, is the subject of this lecture.

00:28.800 --> 00:32.766
Why should such a series need two lags? Because a pendulum

00:32.866 --> 00:36.132
needs two facts: where it is, and how fast it is

00:36.232 --> 00:41.461
moving. Tomorrow's position is today's plus today's velocity, minus a spring

00:41.561 --> 00:46.019
pulling it back, minus friction eating the velocity, plus a gust.

00:46.100 --> 00:50.499
Collect the terms, and the laws of motion become an autoregression of

00:50.599 --> 00:54.542
order two. Friction and the spring decide phi one and phi two.

00:55.633 --> 00:59.286
The memory is written in the roots of the polynomial phi of

00:59.386 --> 01:03.231
z. Complex roots make the response to a single gust swing, and

01:03.331 --> 01:07.366
their distance from the unit circle sets how fast the swing dies.

01:07.466 --> 01:11.393
More friction pushes the roots out and the memory shortens. Let

01:11.493 --> 01:15.675
them cross into the circle, and the pendulum stops forgetting: each

01:15.775 --> 01:19.382
gust is amplified, and the series is no longer stationary.

01:20.466 --> 01:24.412
Given only data, how do we tell which machine produced it?

01:24.512 --> 01:29.364
We read two fingerprints. The autocorrelations of a moving average stop

01:29.464 --> 01:33.271
dead after q lags; those of an autoregression fade away.

01:33.766 --> 01:38.007
The partial autocorrelations do the opposite: for an autoregression

01:38.107 --> 01:40.534
of order p they stop dead after p lags.

01:40.634 --> 01:43.514
The swinging pendulum leaves exactly two bars.

01:44.600 --> 01:48.502
Now forecast. Stand at the last observation, and let the pendulum

01:48.602 --> 01:52.258
run into the future thirty times, each time with fresh gusts.

01:52.333 --> 01:55.979
At first the copies swing together: they still remember where

01:56.079 --> 01:58.927
the pendulum was and how it was moving. Then the

01:59.027 --> 02:02.980
gusts pull them apart. Their average, the optimal forecast, swings

02:03.080 --> 02:05.007
back to the mean and stops there.

02:05.100 --> 02:08.595
The band around it adds up the squared echoes of the gusts

02:08.695 --> 02:12.686
still to come. It widens, and then it freezes at the unconditional

02:12.786 --> 02:16.467
band. Far enough ahead, the future has forgotten the present.

02:17.566 --> 02:21.492
So: an ARMA model is a short recipe for the echoes, a

02:21.592 --> 02:26.962
ratio of two small polynomials. Its fingerprints tell us the orders, and

02:27.062 --> 02:31.520
its forecasts forget the present at the speed of its echoes.
